  "Custom tier": "Tier Make",
  "Daily Nous reports on a retracted Journal of Medical Ethics submission containing multiple fabricated, AI-hallucinated references — complete with fake university affiliations and defunct email addresses — that the author reportedly left uncorrected even after being given a chance to fix proofs. The piece contrasts this with Bioethics, whose automated reference-checking would have caught the problem, and argues the real bottleneck isn't detection technology (one commenter's script flagged the fake citations in under a minute) but unpaid reviewer labor and publishing incentives that reward throughput over scrutiny.": "Daily Nous ej kōnnaan kōn juon submission ñan Journal of Medical Ethics kar bōkḷọk, ewor elōñ reference ko kar make kōṃṃani, AI-hallucinated — kobaik ippān university affiliation ko re waan im email address ko re jako — ri-je eo ear likūt wōt ejjeḷọk kōkāāl meñe ewor iien ñan kōkāāl. Article in ej kōṃṃan compare ñan Bioethics, ijo reference-checking automatic enaaj kar lo problem eo, im ej ba bwe bottleneck e ṃool e jab technology in detection (juon commenter's script ear lo citation ko re waan ilo dikḷọk jān juon minit) ak jerbal an reviewer ro ejjeḷọk aer oṇean im incentive in publishing ko rej lewōj oṇean ñan throughput jān scrutiny.",
  "Data free-riding vs. trillion-dollar AI valuations": "Data Free-Riding im AI Valuation ko rej Trillion Dollar",
  "DeepMind CEO calls for an independent standards body to regulate frontier AI": "DeepMind CEO calls for an independent standards body to regulate frontier AI",
  "Demis Hassabis proposed a FINRA-style regulator for frontier model releases: labs would submit models for review up to 30 days before deployment, voluntary at first, with a path toward mandatory compliance in the US market.": "Demis Hassabis ear kwaḷọk juon regulator ilo wāween FINRA ñan release an model ko e frontier: lab ko renaaj kar letok model ko ñan review ṃae 30 raan ṃokta jān deployment, voluntary ilo jinoin, im ewor iaḷ ñan mandatory compliance ilo market an US.",
  "Denied": "Kar Ban",
  "Deployable policy starter template": "Template in Policy Emaroñ Deploy",
  "Deployable policy starter template at /downloads/agiright-policy-starter.zip — a minimal static site with the full machine-readable layer, ready for Cloudflare Pages or any static host.": "Template in policy emaroñ deploy ilo /downloads/agiright-policy-starter.zip — juon site static e dik wōt ewor an layer machine-readable weeppān, pojak ñan Cloudflare Pages ak jabdewōt static host.",
  "Deploying machine-readable policies on your site": "Deploy Policy ko Machine-Readable ioon Site eo Aṃ",
  "Disclaimer": "Disclaimer",
  "Distinct from human UI, SEO, or plain APIs, the AI Ingestion & Capability Layer gives websites a machine-facing architecture: a Manifest Layer (entry points like /llms.txt and /ai/manifest.json), a Corpus Layer (machine-readable knowledge), a Capability Layer (agent-callable tools), and a Governance Layer (provenance, licensing, versioning). Static-first, upgradeable toward APIs and MCP. Note: in this paper AICL names the ingestion architecture; on this site's protocol pages AICL refers to the AI Content Licensing Layer.": "Oktak jān UI armej, SEO, ak API ko e pidodo, AI Ingestion & Capability Layer ej lewōj ñan website ko juon architecture ej jitōñḷọk ñan machine: Manifest Layer (entry point ko āinwōt /llms.txt im /ai/manifest.json), Corpus Layer (jeḷā machine-readable), Capability Layer (kein jerbal agent emaroñ kūri), im Governance Layer (provenance, laisin, versioning). Static ṃoktata, emaroñ upgrade ñan API ko im MCP. Kōjjeḷā: ilo paper in AICL ej etan architecture in ingestion; ilo page ko in protocol an site in AICL ej jitōñḷọk ñan AI Content Licensing Layer.",
  "Do Large Language Models Possess a Theory of Mind? A Comparative Evaluation Using the Strange Stories Paradigm": "Do Large Language Models Possess a Theory of Mind? A Comparative Evaluation Using the Strange Stories Paradigm",
  "Docs": "Docs",
  "Docs / Examples": "Docs / Waanjoñak",
  "Document library": "Library in Document",
  "Does AI have rights — and which framings are even coherent?": "Ewor ke maroñ an AI — im framing ko ewi rej ṃool im coherent?",
  "Download": "Download",
  "Draft status": "Status in Draft",
  "EU AI Act: What Actually Applies on 2 August 2026": "EU AI Act: What Actually Applies on 2 August 2026",
  "Educational demo: HMAC-SHA256 with a shared secret, showing request-bound, expiring, verifiable license tokens. Production tokens should be signed asymmetrically, with the publisher’s public key discoverable under /.well-known/.": "Demo in katak: HMAC-SHA256 kōn juon secret eṃōj ajej, ej kwaḷọk token in laisin ko lukkuun ekkar ñan request, ewor iien tōpar āneo, im emaroñ verify. Token ko production ren sain kōn asymmetric wāween, im key eo rāllep an publisher en lo ke iuṃwin /.well-known/.",
  "Eight more /topics entries, this time researched by an AI agent rather than curated by hand — same rule as before: every source fetched and confirmed live, summaries an original paraphrase, before anything gets added. 14 entries total.": "Ruwalitōk entry bar juon ilo /topics, ilo iien in AI agent ear kōṃṃan research jab armej kōn pā — ejja kien wōt āinwōt ṃokta: source otemjej kar bōk im kaṃool e mour, summary ko rej paraphrase e kāāl, ṃokta jān jabdewōt kobaik. 14 entry aolep.",
  "Email": "Email",
